<<~ ahu #/invitation>>
! Art Sigils — Example One Folio

The **Tide-Clock Monastery of the Black Lens** occupies a crescent of drowned basalt sea stacks. Its inhabitants read the skies while the sea erases familiar passages and returns an ambiguous reflection.

The Folio develops this single visual inquiry through named studies, counterpoints and material scopes. [[Companion Inscription|ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/art-sigils/example-one-inscription]] presents the compact `art()` invocation. [[Procedure Grammar|ha.ka.ba/lares/api/art-sigils/procedures]] supplies the declarations outside the artistic source.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/folio-sigils>>
!! The complete copyable Folio

Copy the entire **5179-character** block for a receiver that supports extended structured art direction. The opening `art()` sets the whole work; subsequent calls investigate location, tide hazards, instruments, habitation and the abyss. Research URIs inside the final `art-study()` describe **non-depicted** reference sources.

```tiddlywiki
<<art worktype=`concept-art folio` subject=`Tide-Clock Monastery of the Black Lens, drowned basalt towers encircling a deep sea aperture` read=`astronomers seek order in stars and tides; a presence below appears to answer their observations` imagery=`dominant stormlit oblique coastal panorama; flooded observatory monastery, suspended rope bridges, wave caves, bronze tide-clock, polished beacon mirrors, tide stairs, kelp gardens, signal fires, tiny skiffs, dark abyss and leviathan wake; pinned chart and field studies` treatment=`Kay Nielsen mythic ornament on architectural trim; Roger Dean organic stone bridges; Caspar David Friedrich lonely storm horizon; Hiroshi Yoshida layered sea-print light; Arthur Rackham wiry silhouettes in small drawings; Anna Podedworna weathered bronze, wet basalt and inhabited details` composition=`one large elevated sea-stack panorama above uneven smaller tide-stair, beacon, dock, chart and abyss studies; moonlit silver and muted amber, cold green water, black rock and vermilion ropes on distinct papers` relation=`the clock suggests a predictable lunar cycle yet floodwater erases the landing route; polished mirrors map the stars while the Black Lens returns an unexplained glint; inhabited shelter persists above unseen danger` depiction-scope=`folio alone fills frame; figures inhabit embedded views, not external studio; research links and artist names stay off the pictured sheets`>>
<<art-study question=`Where does the monastery stand?` form=`high oblique painterly sea-stack panorama` proposal=`a crescent of wave-cut basalt pinnacles encloses one black marine hollow; causeways, ledges and narrow shelves sustain a flooded observatory, beacons and landing slips` status=`fictional geography, not surveyed coastline`>>
<<art-facture scope=`main panorama` support=`ivory cold-press watercolor sheet` medium=`glazed indigo and green watercolor with pearl gouache` handling=`layered sea haze and distant weather; detailed dry-stone silhouettes balanced by luminous marine air`>>
<<art-study question=`When can anyone climb the tide stairs?` form=`vellum route elevation and embodied landing sketch` proposal=`stone stairs reach the dock at ebb but lose their lowest flights under a rising surge; skiff mooring drifts beneath the cut rock` status=`hazardous crossing`>>
<<art-counterpoint first=`tidal clockwork claims regular access` second=`the landing stair disappears beneath surf` tension=`prediction does not confer safety` evidence=`matching step shapes, flood marks and a mooring ring visible in both views`>>
<<art-facture scope=`landing chart and stair study` support=`translucent vellum over pale blue-gray rag paper` medium=`graphite, wash and muted vermilion chalk` handling=`partly erased waterline and corrected foot-route share identifiable ledges`>>
<<art-study question=`What does the beacon measure?` form=`tower section and mirrored instrument detail` proposal=`bronze tide clock, ceramic markers and polished reflectors aim toward the horizon and astronomical bearings; bells and mirrors also face the sea hollow` status=`ritual and practical purposes coexist`>>
<<art-counterpoint first=`beacon mirrors reflect constellations` second=`the Black Lens returns a deep-sea glint` tension=`watcher and watched exchange positions` evidence=`aligned sightlines, contradictory reflections and an unaccounted-for lower gleam`>>
<<art-study question=`Who keeps the sanctuary alive?` form=`ink-wash dock, kelp garden and workshop vignette` proposal=`bell tenders, monks, fishers and salvagers tend rope bridges, herb terraces, glazed markers and repaired boats` status=`small occupied refuge`>>
<<art-counterpoint first=`cultivated kelp garden and dry refuges` second=`storm wreckage and a distant leviathan wake` tension=`care and danger inhabit the same coastline` evidence=`repaired lines and lanterns beside gouged planking and disturbed surf`>>
<<art-facture scope=`beacon and inhabited dock studies` support=`smooth Bristol ink and pale sea-gray paper` medium=`bronze-and-ceramic gouache, fine ink and scratched highlights` handling=`credible wet materials, intricate ornament and needle-fine rigging`>>
<<art-study question=`What hides beneath the Black Lens?` form=`dark charcoal abyssal bathymetry and reflective water sketch` proposal=`a descending seabed funnel holds a round black absence; readings suggest sinkhole, leviathan eye or ancient instrument` status=`unresolved identity`>>
<<art-facture scope=`Black Lens underside` support=`cool charcoal stock and translucent blue ink overlay` medium=`compressed graphite, chalk, dark wash and sparse silver ink` handling=`heavy negative space, one uncertain iris-ring, no explanatory label`>>
<<art-study question=`Which external sources inform the setting?` form=`NON-DEPICTED references: Skellig Michael https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/757 ; NOAA bathymetry https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/maps/bathymetry/ ; Hiroshi Yoshida print https://asia-archive.si.edu/object/S2003.8.3459/` proposal=`borrow exposed monastic access, seafloor visualization and layered marine light without depicting source photographs or names` status=`research bearings, not setting canon`>>
```

This extended form contains webpage reference URIs rather than direct Midjourney image assets. The compact Inscription offers the preferred Midjourney-first form; the Folio may benefit from receivers with longer context and retrieval.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/research>>
!! Maritime and artistic bearings

* [[Skellig Michael World Heritage|https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/757]] — exposed monastic occupation amid steep maritime terrain
* [[NOAA Bathymetry Viewer|https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/maps/bathymetry/]] — seabed shape and underwater spatial representation
* [[Hiroshi Yoshida — Sailing Boats: Mist|https://asia-archive.si.edu/object/S2003.8.3459/]] — printmaking approaches to luminous coastal atmosphere

Kay Nielsen guides ornamental rhythm; Roger Dean lends improbable architecture; Caspar David Friedrich supplies solitary weather; Hiroshi Yoshida offers layered marine light; Arthur Rackham shapes wiry line; Anna Podedworna informs believable fantasy materials. Those bounded bearings remain distinct from a wholesale imitation of any one artist.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/depiction>>
!! Pictorial and fictional boundaries

The frame contains a single folio with one dominant oblique panorama and supporting studies on unequal paper supports. Monks, fishers and salvagers appear only *inside* depicted studies. Source photographs, printed artist names, studio furniture, navigational measurements and definite explanations of the Black Lens do not enter the painted work.

The clock describes a plausible cycle without guaranteeing safe crossing. Mirrors, tide marks and the dark water invite rival explanations without proving an ancient machine or a living abyss. The monastery and its lore occupy a fictional setting; the terrestrial sources offer artistic bearings rather than canonical geography.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/aftermath>>
!! Aftermath — observed relation and withheld certainty

The tide-clock provides order while its stairs lose access; the beacon studies outward while the central aperture appears to answer; gardens and repairs persist alongside peril. Each contrast draws meaning through visible relationships. An implied leviathan does not require a confirmed leviathan, and a reflected light need not settle the Black Lens's identity.
<<~/ahu>>
